--- a/docs/LANGUAGE_PACKS.md
+++ b/docs/LANGUAGE_PACKS.md
@@ -16,3 +16,11 @@ Imported base-language packs apply to regional variants. A specific variant pack
 Dictionary membership is not grammar analysis. A wordlist-only language can flag unfamiliar words but does not have morphological suggestion generation unless a Hunspell/provider engine supplies it. No English fallback is silently used for other languages.
 
 English OCR is bundled separately from proofreading. To recognize another script, supply appropriate Tesseract trained data and add an OCR document/provider extension using that model. Installing a spelling wordlist alone does not install an OCR model.
+
+## How Hunspell pairs are checked
+
+Hunspell `.aff` + `.dic` pairs are read by a bounded reader (`src/core/algorithms/hunspell.js`), not by a parse-time expander. Classic Hunspell tooling expands every affix rule for every dictionary word up front; for morphologically rich tables — Romance verbs with clitic continuation classes, for example — that explodes into millions of generated forms, freezes the checker worker, and can push the browser past its object-key limits ("Too many properties to enumerate"). Proofloom instead parses the affix table into a capped model and applies rules in reverse, per query: surface forms, multi-level continuation chains, and prefix/suffix cross-products all resolve, with flat memory and time for every language.
+
+Safety caps keep any pair from stalling the editor: up to 4,000 affix rules, 25,000 affix entries, and 400,000 dictionary words are parsed; each membership query is bounded. A pair that exceeds the caps (or uses a legacy encoding) still works as a surface-form wordlist, without morphology. Suggestions for every language come from the shared candidate models — deletion index, phonetic buckets, keyboard adjacency, split/join, and extra-character deletion — run over the pack's own vocabulary, then ranked by the weighted scorer.
+
+Every check also runs under a time budget. If a check cannot finish within it, the worker returns everything found so far and marks the result as degraded; the editor shows a notice instead of losing the text. This is a safety net — with the bounded reader it should never engage.
